# Developing

Day-to-day tasks: running code, running tests, adding dependencies, debugging.

## Running code with uv

uv runs Python through the workspace virtual environment automatically. No need to activate the venv manually unless you prefer to.

```bash
# Run a script
uv run python path/to/script.py

# Run a module
uv run python -m module_name

# Run a CLI tool from a dependency
uv run pytest
uv run pyright
uv run ruff check .
```

To activate the venv so commands don't need the `uv run` prefix:

```bash
source .venv/bin/activate
python path/to/script.py
deactivate  # when finished
```

Activating helps in an interactive session. `uv run` is cleaner for one-off commands and scripts.

## Running a specific app

After `make new-app NAME=foo`, the app lives at `apps/foo/`.

{% if app_framework == "fastapi" -%}
Run locally with hot reload:

```bash
uv run uvicorn foo.main:app --reload --port 8080
```

Then hit `http://localhost:8080/healthz` to confirm it's up.
{%- else -%}
Run the entrypoint:

```bash
uv run python -m foo.main
```
{%- endif %}

## Running tests

```bash
# All tests in the workspace
make test

# Tests for one app
uv run pytest apps/foo/tests/

# A single test file
uv run pytest apps/foo/tests/test_main.py

# A single test function
uv run pytest apps/foo/tests/test_main.py::test_healthz_returns_ok

# Stop on first failure
uv run pytest -x

# Drop into pdb on failure
uv run pytest --pdb

# Show captured print output
uv run pytest -s

# Filter by test name substring
uv run pytest -k "invoice"
```

For async tests, `asyncio_mode = "auto"` in `pyproject.toml` removes the need for `@pytest.mark.asyncio` markers on every test.

## Adding dependencies

To the workspace dev group (tools like `pytest-mock`, `pytest-asyncio`):

```bash
uv add --group dev pytest-mock
```

To a specific app:

```bash
uv add --package foo httpx
```

To the shared library (visible to all apps):

```bash
uv add --package shared pydantic-extras
```

After adding, commit both `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock`.

## Updating dependencies

```bash
# Upgrade all dependencies within their version constraints
uv lock --upgrade

# Upgrade one specific package
uv lock --upgrade-package pydantic
```

After `uv lock`, run `uv sync` to install the new versions, run `make ci` to confirm nothing broke, and commit `uv.lock`.

## Working with secrets locally

{% if use_gcp -%}
Application Default Credentials let the same `get_secret()` call work locally and in Cloud Run.

First-time setup (already in `bootstrap.sh`):

```bash
gcloud auth application-default login
gcloud config set project {{ gcp_project_dev }}
```

After that, `get_secret("PORTAL_API_KEY")` reads from `{{ gcp_project_dev }}` using your credentials.

**Creating a secret** (do this before calling `get_secret` for the first time):

```bash
# Create the secret
gcloud secrets create PORTAL_API_KEY --replication-policy="automatic"

# Set its value (prompts for input)
echo -n "the-secret-value" | gcloud secrets versions add PORTAL_API_KEY --data-file=-
```

Grant your app's **runtime service account** access after deploying:

```bash
gcloud secrets add-iam-policy-binding PORTAL_API_KEY \
  --member="serviceAccount:<runtime-sa>@{{ gcp_project_dev }}.iam.gserviceaccount.com" \
  --role="roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor"
```

> **Runtime SA vs deploy SA.** The project has two CI/CD service accounts created by Terraform: one that deploys containers (`deploy`), one that runs Terraform (`terraform`). Neither of these is the identity your app runs *as* in Cloud Run. Cloud Run services run as a separate **runtime service account** — one you create per service with only the permissions that service needs (Secret Manager access, Pub/Sub, etc.). If you don't specify one, Cloud Run falls back to the Compute Engine default SA, which has broad project-level permissions. Create a dedicated runtime SA for each service and pass it to `gcloud run deploy` with `--service-account=<runtime-sa>@<project>.iam.gserviceaccount.com`. The specific SA name and roles depend on what your service needs.

For local-only values that aren't secrets (test fixtures, mock URLs, throwaway config), create `.env` at the project root:

```
PORTAL_URL=http://localhost:8080
MAX_RETRIES=1
```

`.env` is gitignored. Never put real secrets in `.env`; use Secret Manager.
{%- else -%}
Use `.env` at the project root for local config:

```
PORTAL_URL=http://localhost:8080
MAX_RETRIES=1
```

`.env` is gitignored. `pydantic-settings` reads it automatically at app startup.
{%- endif %}

{% if include_docker -%}
## Building and running a container locally

Build the image:

```bash
make build NAME=foo
```

This produces an image tagged `{{ project_name }}-foo:local`.

Run it:

```bash
docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 \
  -e GCP_PROJECT={{ gcp_project_dev }} \
  -v ~/.config/gcloud:/root/.config/gcloud \
  {{ project_name }}-foo:local
```

The volume mount makes your gcloud ADC available inside the container, so Secret Manager calls work the same way they would in Cloud Run.

{% endif -%}
## Pre-commit hooks

Hooks run on every `git commit`. If a hook modifies files (ruff's autofix, for example), the commit is aborted; re-stage the modified files and commit again.

```bash
# Run all hooks against all files (after editing hook config)
uv run pre-commit run --all-files

# Update hook versions to latest
uv run pre-commit autoupdate
```

To bypass hooks for an emergency commit:

```bash
git commit --no-verify
```

Use rarely. The hooks keep `main` clean; bypassing pushes cleanup work to whoever notices later.

## VS Code

The repo commits `.vscode/settings.json` and `.vscode/extensions.json`. On first opening the project, VS Code prompts to install the recommended extensions.

Python interpreter selection should auto-detect `.venv/bin/python`. If it doesn't: `Cmd+Shift+P` → "Python: Select Interpreter" → choose the workspace `.venv`.

To run tests from the editor: open the Testing view in the sidebar (test-tube icon). Run individual tests or files via the play button.

To debug: open a Python file, click in the gutter to set a breakpoint, then `F5`. Edit `.vscode/launch.json` for a non-default entrypoint.

## Common troubleshooting

**`uv: command not found`.** Re-run `./scripts/bootstrap.sh`, which installs uv. Manual fallback: `curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh`.

**`ModuleNotFoundError` for a workspace package.** Run `uv sync`. This is the symptom when dependencies are out of date or a new app was scaffolded but not synced.

**Pre-commit hook fails on commit.** Run `make fix` (ruff autofix and format), re-stage, commit again. For pyright failures, run `make typecheck` to see the full output.

**Pyright reports errors on third-party imports.** The cause: missing type stubs for the package. Try `uv add --group dev types-<package>`. If no stubs exist, the warning is acceptable (the template downgrades `reportMissingTypeStubs` to a warning, not an error).

**Tests pass locally but fail in CI.** Most often, `uv.lock` isn't committed or a fixture path differs between environments. Run `uv sync` locally, then `make ci` to mirror the CI checks exactly.

{% if use_gcp -%}
**`google.api_core.exceptions.PermissionDenied` from `get_secret()`.** Re-authenticate: `gcloud auth application-default login`. Then confirm the project: `gcloud config get-value project`.

**`docker push` fails with auth error.** Run `gcloud auth configure-docker us-central1-docker.pkg.dev --quiet`.

**Cloud Run service starts but no logs appear in Cloud Logging.** The app needs to call `configure_logging()` once at startup. Without it, structlog writes nowhere visible.
{%- endif %}
